+    "id": "SUP-BS-OZONE-CLIMATE",
+    "gapIds": [
+      "WG-BS-SL024"
+    ],
+    "subjectName": "Basic Science",
+    "termLabel": "1st Term",
+    "title": "Depletion of the ozone layer and its effects (corrected: ozone depletion is not the greenhouse effect)",
+    "origin": "New teaching material prepared under the owner's 15 October 2026 authorisation to use internet equivalents; not recovered school-source text. It supersedes, in delivery, the original First-Term row 'DEPLETION OT OZONE LAYER', which says that ozone gives a cooling effect, that depletion lets more ozone collect in the lower air and causes the greenhouse effect, and that carbon dioxide depletes the ozone layer. The original row is preserved unchanged in the audit record.",
+    "sources": [
+      {
+        "title": "Are the ozone hole and global warming related? — NASA Earth Observatory",
+        "url": "https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/blogs/climateqa/are-the-ozone-hole-and-global-warming-related"
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "Montreal Protocol — UNEP Ozone Secretariat",
+        "url": "https://ozone.unep.org/node/132"
+      }
+    ],
+    "body": "Objectives: locate the ozone layer; state its importance; name the substances that deplete it and their effects; separate ozone depletion from the greenhouse effect; state control measures.\n\nTeaching:\n1. Location. The atmosphere has layers. The ozone layer is mainly in the stratosphere, above the troposphere where we live and where weather happens.\n2. Importance. Ozone in the stratosphere absorbs most of the Sun's harmful ultraviolet (UV) radiation. Because it absorbs UV, it warms the stratosphere; it does not cool the Earth's surface.\n3. Cause of depletion. Chemicals made by people, mainly chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) and halons, rise to the stratosphere. Sunlight breaks them up and the chlorine or bromine released destroys ozone molecules. Sources were old refrigerators and air conditioners, aerosol sprays, foam and some fire extinguishers. The Montreal Protocol of 1987 phased out these substances and the ozone layer is slowly recovering.\n4. Effects of depletion. More UV reaches the ground. It can cause sunburn, skin cancer, eye cataracts and a weaker immune system in people, and can harm crops and sea life.\n5. Not the same as global warming. Ozone depletion does not cause global warming. Global warming is the rise in average surface temperature caused mainly by greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide and methane that trap heat in the lower atmosphere. Carbon dioxide does not deplete the ozone layer. The two problems have a link only because CFCs are also greenhouse gases. Deforestation and bush burning raise carbon dioxide and pollute the air; they add to global warming, not directly to ozone destruction.\n6. Control. Support the Montreal Protocol and use ozone-friendly products; have old refrigerators and air conditioners collected and handled by trained people so that the refrigerant is not released; do not buy products that contain banned ozone-depleting chemicals. To reduce global warming, reduce fossil-fuel burning and bush burning and plant trees.\n7. Protect yourself from UV: use shade, a hat and sun-safe clothing in strong sun.\n\nWorked example: A scrap dealer is about to cut open an old refrigerator. Ada explains that the refrigerant could escape and damage the ozone layer, so a trained technician must remove it first.\n\nPractice (questions):\n1. In which layer of the atmosphere is the ozone layer found?\n2. State two functions of the ozone layer.\n3. Name two substances that deplete the ozone layer and two sources of them.\n4. State three effects of ozone depletion.\n5. Explain why carbon dioxide and CFCs are not treated as the same problem.\n6. State three control measures.\n\nAnswers (marking guide): 1. Stratosphere (1). 2. Absorbs harmful UV (1); protects life, skin and eyes (1). 3. CFCs and halons (2); refrigerators, air conditioners, aerosols, foam, fire extinguishers (any two, 2). 4. Any three (3): sunburn, skin cancer, cataracts, weaker immunity, crop and sea-life damage. 5. CO2 traps heat and warms the surface but does not destroy ozone; CFCs destroy ozone and also trap some heat (2). 6. Any three (3): Montreal Protocol, safe disposal of old fridges and air conditioners, ozone-friendly products, not releasing refrigerant."
+  },
+  {
+    "id": "SUP-BS-MAGNET-POLES",
+    "gapIds": [
+      "WG-BS-SL278"
+    ],
+    "subjectName": "Basic Science",
+    "termLabel": "2nd Term",
+    "title": "Magnetism: poles, the compass and the care of magnets (corrected pole naming)",
+    "origin": "New teaching material prepared under the owner's 15 October 2026 authorisation to use internet equivalents; not recovered school-source text. It supersedes, in delivery, the original Second-Term row 'Magnetism-', which says that the end of a magnet pointing to the geographic north pole is the south pole of the magnet. By the standard convention that end is the north (north-seeking) pole. The original row is preserved unchanged in the audit record.",
+    "sources": [
+      {
+        "title": "Earth as a Magnet — LibreTexts Physical Science",
+        "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Courses/Coalinga_College/Physical_Science_for_Educators_(CID:_PHYS_14)/12:_Magnetism/12.04:_Earth_as_a_Magnet"
+      }
+    ],
+    "body": "Objectives: state the law of magnetic poles; name the poles of a freely hanging magnet correctly; detect poles with a compass; describe a magnetic field; state how to care for magnets.\n\nTeaching:\n1. A magnet attracts iron and steel. Lodestone is a natural magnet. Every magnet has two poles, found near its ends, where the force is strongest.\n2. Law of poles: like poles repel; unlike poles attract.\n3. Naming the poles. A bar magnet that is free to swing comes to rest pointing roughly north and south. The end that points to the geographic north is called the north-seeking pole, or north pole, of the magnet. The end that points to the geographic south is the south pole. So the north pole of a magnet points towards the Earth's geographic north. The Earth itself acts like a huge magnet: the magnetic pole of the Earth near the geographic north attracts the north pole of a compass needle, so that Earth pole is magnetically a south pole.\n4. Detecting poles. Bring a compass near a bar magnet. The end of the magnet that repels the north end of the compass needle is a north pole. You can also hang the magnet on a thread and see which end points north.\n5. Magnetic field: the region around a magnet where its force acts. Field lines leave the north pole and enter the south pole. The field is strongest near the poles. Iron filings sprinkled on paper over a magnet show the pattern.\n6. Care of magnets: store them in pairs with unlike poles together and a soft-iron keeper across the ends; keep them dry, cool and away from heat, hammering and dropping, which weaken them. Keep magnets away from phones, cards and watches.\n7. Uses: compasses, loudspeakers, microphones, electric motors and generators, door catches, and lifting iron objects.\n\nWorked example: A compass needle swings to point at the right-hand end of a bar magnet. The needle's north end is attracted, so the right-hand end of the magnet is its south pole.\n\nPractice (questions):\n1. State the law of magnetic poles.\n2. Which end of a freely hanging magnet points to the geographic north: its north pole or south pole?\n3. Explain why the north end of a compass needle points to the Earth's geographic north.\n4. Describe how to find the north pole of an unmarked magnet using a compass.\n5. State three ways of caring for magnets.\n6. State two uses of magnets.\n\nAnswers (marking guide): 1. Like poles repel; unlike poles attract (2). 2. The north pole (the north-seeking pole) (1). 3. The Earth acts like a magnet; the magnetic pole near the geographic north attracts the needle's north end (2). 4. Bring the compass near; the end of the magnet that repels the compass north end is the north pole (2). 5. Any three (3): keepers, store in pairs, dry place, away from heat, avoid hammering or dropping. 6. Any two (2): compass, loudspeaker, motor, generator, door catch."
+  },
+  {
+    "id": "SUP-BS-ELECTRIC-CIRCUITS",
