+    "body": "Objectives: define STIs, HIV and AIDS; state how HIV is and is not spread; state prevention methods; explain testing and treatment; show respect for people living with HIV; correct dated notes on disease classification.\n\nTeaching:\n1. Sexually transmitted infections (STIs) spread mainly through sexual contact. Examples are gonorrhoea, syphilis, chlamydia and HIV. Many bacterial STIs can be cured when treated early by a health worker; viral ones such as HIV are managed with treatment. Some STIs show no signs, so a person cannot tell by looking. Do not buy drugs for self-treatment; go to a clinic.\n2. HIV (human immunodeficiency virus) attacks the immune system. AIDS (acquired immune deficiency syndrome) is the late stage when the immune system is badly weakened. HIV is spread through unprotected sex, infected blood (unsafe transfusion, shared needles, razors or other skin-piercing tools) and from a mother to her baby during pregnancy, birth or breastfeeding.\n3. HIV is not spread by handshakes, hugging, sharing plates or cups, toilets, swimming, mosquitoes or sleeping in the same room.\n4. Testing: only a test at a health facility shows whether a person has HIV; signs alone cannot tell. Testing comes with counselling.\n5. Treatment: antiretroviral therapy (ART) is a lifelong daily treatment. A person taking it properly can stay healthy and live a long life. A person on ART whose test shows the virus at an undetectable level does not pass HIV to a sexual partner. A woman living with HIV who is on ART and under care can have a baby with a very low chance of passing HIV on. So the old advice not to become pregnant is replaced by: seek care early at a clinic.\n6. Prevention: not having sex is the surest way to avoid sexual transmission; condoms used correctly every time lower the risk of HIV and many STIs; do not share needles, razors or blades; accept blood only from tested sources; medical male circumcision done by a trained health worker in a clean setting lowers a man's risk, whereas cutting with unsterilised tools raises it; health workers wear gloves and cover wounds.\n7. Stigma harms. People living with HIV deserve care, privacy and respect. Support is practical (company, care, going to the clinic), not only prayer.\n8. Correcting related notes on family health: smallpox no longer occurs because it was eradicated, so it is not a current communicable disease; snake-bite is an injury, not an infectious disease; many non-communicable diseases such as stroke or heart disease are not passed from person to person.\n\nWorked example: Bola hears a rumour that she can get HIV from sharing a cup with a classmate. She explains that HIV is not spread that way, and tells her friend that anyone worried about HIV should be tested at a clinic and, if positive, can begin ART.\n\nPractice (questions):\n1. Give the full meaning of HIV, AIDS and STI.\n2. State three ways HIV is spread and three ways it is not spread.\n3. Why can a person not tell by looking whether someone has HIV?\n4. What is ART and why is it important?\n5. State four ways of preventing HIV.\n6. Why is it wrong to isolate or insult a person living with HIV?\n\nAnswers (marking guide): 1. Human immunodeficiency virus; acquired immune deficiency syndrome; sexually transmitted infection (3). 2. Any three spread: unprotected sex, infected blood or shared sharp tools, mother to baby (3); any three not: handshake, shared plates, toilets, swimming, mosquitoes (3). 3. Many STIs and HIV show no signs for a long time; only a test shows (1). 4. Lifelong daily treatment that lets a person stay healthy and, when the virus is undetectable, prevents sexual spread (2). 5. Any four (4): not having sex, condoms, not sharing sharp tools, tested blood, trained circumcision, early care. 6. Stigma stops people getting tests and care; HIV is not spread by everyday contact (2). Teacher: do not give personal medical advice; refer questions to a health worker."
