+    "body": "Objectives: define drug, medicine, drug use, misuse and abuse; state the main types of drugs; define dependence, tolerance and withdrawal; explain the dangers of self-medication and fake medicines; state safe choices.\n\nTeaching:\n1. A drug is any substance that changes how the body or mind works. A medicine is a drug used to prevent, treat or cure illness. Use means taking a medicine as a health worker or the label directs. Misuse means taking a medicine in a wrong way, for example without advice, in the wrong amount, or for a purpose it was not given for. Abuse means using drugs or substances in a harmful way, often for the effect on the mind.\n2. Main types: stimulants (speed the body up), depressants (slow the body down), cannabis, inhalants, hallucinogens and prescription medicines. Some medicines, such as certain sleeping or calming tablets (for example diazepam, sold as valium), are prescription medicines. They are legal only when a qualified person prescribes them, and misuse is dangerous. Illegal drugs include cocaine and heroin.\n3. Dependence (addiction): the body or mind feels a strong need for the drug. Tolerance: the person needs more of the drug to get the same effect. Withdrawal: unpleasant illness when the drug is stopped. These are signs of dependence and need help from a health worker, not punishment.\n4. Self-medication means buying and taking medicines without a health worker's advice. Dangers: the wrong medicine or amount, side effects, hiding a serious illness, antibiotic resistance, and dangerous mixtures. Use antibiotics only when prescribed. Never share prescription medicines.\n5. Fake and expired medicines are common. Buy from licensed pharmacies, check the expiry date and the NAFDAC registration number, and where a pack carries a scratch code use NAFDAC's Mobile Authentication Service. Herbal products can also be harmful when contaminated or taken in the wrong amount; ask a health worker.\n6. Reasons people abuse drugs: peer pressure, curiosity, stress, easy availability and trouble at home. Safe choices: learn the facts, say no firmly, keep busy, talk to a trusted adult or counsellor, and avoid places where drugs are used.\n7. NAFDAC checks the safety and quality of foods and medicines; NDLEA enforces laws on illegal drugs and offers counselling and rehabilitation programmes.\n\nWorked example: Ife has a headache and her friend offers her some tablets from an unmarked packet. Ife refuses, tells her mother and asks the pharmacist for advice.\n\nPractice (questions):\n1. Differentiate between drug use, misuse and abuse.\n2. Name four types of drugs.\n3. Define dependence, tolerance and withdrawal.\n4. State three dangers of self-medication.\n5. State two ways to check that a medicine is genuine.\n6. State two functions of NAFDAC and two of NDLEA.\n\nAnswers (marking guide): 1. Use is as directed; misuse is wrong or unprescribed use of a medicine; abuse is harmful use for the effect (3). 2. Any four (4): stimulants, depressants, cannabis, inhalants, hallucinogens, prescription medicines. 3. Strong need for the drug; needing more for the same effect; illness when stopped (3). 4. Any three (3): wrong medicine or dose, side effects, hides illness, antibiotic resistance, dangerous mixtures. 5. Any two (2): licensed pharmacy, expiry date, NAFDAC number, scratch-code check. 6. NAFDAC: checks quality and safety of food and medicines, registers products (2); NDLEA: enforces drug laws, counselling and rehabilitation (2)."
